{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And his heart went numb.</b> From the language of (Lamentations 3:49) ״מאין הפוגות״ — that is, there is no cessation of tears; its meaning is that the movement of his heart ceased and he was like a dead man. And so too by way of nature, when joy comes suddenly upon a person, he faints and is like a dead man. The reason for this is that the heart opens and expands suddenly, and the natural heat within the heart goes out from it and spreads to the exterior of the body, and then the heart within remains cold, and as a result he comes to fainting. And this phenomenon is found mostly among the elderly and among people of weak strength when joy comes upon them suddenly. And like the matter that the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in tractate Ketubot: there was a certain woman sitting and sifting flour. Her husband lifted his eyes and saw her. Her heart swooned and her spirit departed. They said: This poor woman guarded herself in vain. He begged mercy for her and she revived. For through the abundance of joy that came to her suddenly upon seeing her husband, her heart went numb and her spirit departed."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The Aramaic anecdote from Ketubot is rendered faithfully into English. The printed citation refers to b. Ketubot 62b–63a (the story of Rabbi Akiva's wife)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:33.320100574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}